---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import JournalNav from '../../components/JournalNav.astro';

const lang = 'en';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Cite | Ayamatma"
  description="Citation format for Ayamatma: Journal of Vedantic Studies."
  lang={lang}
>
  <section class="section stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Citation</p>
    <h1 class="section-title">How to Cite Ayamatma Articles</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      Each formal article should have a stable landing page and complete bibliographic metadata.
    </p>
    <JournalNav />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>General citation format</h2>
      <p>
        Once the journal is live, each formal article page should include a recommended citation.
        A typical citation will use this structure:
      </p>

      <pre><code>Author Last Name, Initials. (Year). Title of article. Ayamatma: Journal of Vedantic Studies, volume(issue), Article number. https://doi.org/10.xxxx/ayamatma.year.article</code></pre>

      <h2>Example placeholder</h2>
      <p>
        The following is a template only. It does not claim an actual DOI:
      </p>

      <pre><code>Boggavarapu, K. (2026). Why Ayamatma exists. Ayamatma: Journal of Vedantic Studies, 1(1), Article 1. https://doi.org/10.xxxx/ayamatma.2026.001</code></pre>

      <h2>Landing page requirements</h2>
      <p>
        A DOI should resolve to the article landing page, not merely to a file. The landing page should
        display the article title, author, abstract, keywords, article type, review status, date, volume,
        issue, article number, license, citation, and full-text access.
      </p>

      <h2>Current DOI status</h2>
      <p>
        DOIs are planned but not yet assigned. Any placeholder DOI shown on this site is a model for future
        citation practice, not a registered identifier.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
